# Host-toolchain workaround, not a behaviour change: osfmk/ipc/mach_port.c
# asserts on a const local, which is not an integer constant expression in C.
# Xcode 15's clang folds it, Xcode 14's does not. See scripts/xnu-clang for the
# other two arguments this host has to rewrite.
#
# xnu-8796 (iOS 16.5) dropped both anchors: mach_port.c no longer asserts on a
# const local, and the libkern/os/log.c XNUPOST address comparisons are gone, so
# these two have nothing left to rewrite. The files stay on disk -- the host gap
# is still real, and a 8796 build that trips on a static_assert of the same
# shape gets fixed here.
#zzzz-host-clang-static-assert.patch
# Same class, same reason: libkern/os/log.c asserts that two macro aliases have
# the same address, which Xcode 14's clang will not fold into a constant.
#zzzz-host-clang-xnupost-assert.patch
# Third host-toolchain gap, this one in iig rather than clang: the iig shipped
# with older Xcodes emits an unguarded IORPCMessageFromMach() call into every
# generated *.iig.cpp _Dispatch(), while iokit/DriverKit/IORPC.h (restored by
# prepare-xnu-source.py from Apple's public source) declares it only for user
# space. Adds the kernel-side prototype to the generated file.
# Not reachable from the DriverKit headers themselves: they are untracked in
# .cache/xnu-work, so a device patch cannot touch them.
# 8796 half: IOUserServer.cpp already defines IORPCMessageFromMach(msg, reply),
# so the old "rename IORPCMessageFromMachReply and export it" hunk is gone and
# only the iig Makefile injection remains.
zzzz-host-iig-iorpc-export.patch
# Match-pass trace for the PE_lockdown_iokit hard-freeze (2026-11-01): the
# emmc1 kernel freezes with timer interrupts dead between the
# "PE_lockdown_iokit" and "bsd_init enter" panel stages, i.e. inside
# StartIOKitMatching's first match pass. These marks put every personality
# match/probe/start plus the ArmDTPlatform checkpoints on the panel so the
# spinning driver is visible in a photograph. boot-emmc1's only delta vs the
# working boot-smp2 is the kernel binary itself (FD/DTB/ESP verified
# identical, ADT differs only in random-seed), so a layout-sensitive latent
# bug in the match path is the prime suspect.
zzz-match-trace.patch
# Root-match observation (2026-11-03): permanent counters for the FIRST match
# pass. The panel history only keeps a few rows, so the root pass (the one that
# decides whether ArmDTPlatform runs) is always overwritten by the later
# IOResources/IOReportHub passes -- which is why every photograph since match3
# shows "DT cfg=0" without saying whether findDrivers() returned nothing for
# /device-tree or returned it and dropped the candidate. Diagnosis only; delete
# together with zzz-match-trace.patch once the root pass is understood.
zzz-match-observe.patch
# Stage B of the same instrumentation, as its own file so the counter
# definitions cannot be lost again by a prepare re-clone (the first version was
# only in .cache/xnu-work and the link failed with "_r11_reg_enter, referenced
# from _draw in libBootProgress.a"). Adds the registerService()/startMatching()/
# doServiceMatch() counters that rows 2 and 3 paint. Applied immediately after
# zzz-match-observe.patch because its line numbers assume that state.
zzz-match-stage-b.patch
# The stage after that: with the match pass contained, the 16.5 panel stopped at
# mac_policy_initmach, inside the start() of a prelinked security-extension kext
# (AMFI / "failed to get mix-n-match policy: 2"), on the boot thread. Skipping the
# whole pass moved the stage to trust_cache_init and then panic'd on
# "image4 interface not available", because trust_cache_runtime_init() needs the
# img4if/amfi globals those kexts register -- so the switch is now a bit mask that
# names one kext (see the patch header). Bisect tool, not a fix.
zzz-skip-security-extensions.patch
# The other half of that bisect (2026-11-03): with the pass skipped the boot
# cannot get past trust_cache_init, and trust_cache_init sits BEFORE
# PE_lockdown_iokit in kernel_bootstrap() (startup.c: 723 mac_policy_initmach,
# 742 trust_cache_runtime_init, 777 PE_lockdown_iokit). That ordering is why
# every image up to boot-rootreg1 painted all-zero ROOT/REG/SM/DSM rows: the
# first match pass had not run yet, it was not skipped by registerService().
# r11-tc-skip=1 makes both trust-cache entry points return early when
# img4if/amfi are missing, so the boot can reach StartIOKitMatching() without
# the security extensions. Guarded on those globals: mask=0 boots unchanged.
zzz-trustcache-skip.patch
# Second half of the same switch: KLDBootstrap's pass is not the only way the
# security extensions start. Once the platform expert publishes the top level DT
# nodes, IOService::registerService runs a synchronous match for each of them, and
# /defaults matches AppleImage4 (IONameMatch "defaults", provider IOPlatformDevice).
# Its start function stores into its own __DATA_CONST, which arm_vm_prot_finalize()
# has already made read-only by then (machine_lockdown runs before PE_lockdown_iokit
# in this port), so the store is a level-3 write permission fault in the kernel
# static region. This patch applies the same mask inside OSKext::start(), which lets
# matching walk past the security kexts and on to the /cpus nubs.
zzz-skip-security-start.patch
